body:
A stock Moonrun launcher cannot preserve original console Ctrl-C for an owned Node child on Windows. The parent observes Interrupt, but its graceful cancellation delivers Ctrl-Break, so a genuine Node-owned Moon-Lander host reports Quit/131 rather than Interrupt/130. Cleanup succeeds; this request concerns event identity and a supported public delivery capability.

Observed on Windows GitHub Actions with Node24.14.1 and both official toolchains:

- compiler0.10.16+b1a0e846e, Moon/Moonrun0.1.20261020, commit2284bd2;
- compiler0.10.17+d971670cd, Moon/Moonrun0.1.20261023, commit3778465.

## Actual runtime evidence

[Public Windows qualification job](https://github.com/moonrockz/moon-lander/actions/runs/41109314843/job/124247830952), [two-stock receipts](https://github.com/moonrockz/moon-lander/actions/runs/41109314843/artifacts/13001914181), tested source9e975ac7d4bee9e82ae10d43cebf4124993d5695.

Both stocks give the same observations:

| Controlled isolated-console case | Genuine Node host | Parent / cleanup |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Direct Node, ordinary Ctrl-C | Interrupt, finalizer130, completion130 | Node reaped |
| Moonrun parent receives Ctrl-C | Quit, finalizer131, completion131 | Parent records Interrupt, child131, force=false, parent bits0xc000013a |
| Moonrun parent receives targeted Ctrl-Break | Quit, finalizer131, completion131 | Child reaped, worker/service/finalizer complete |
| Owned Node payload ignores Ctrl-Break | Actual ignored-Break acknowledgement | Child exit1 after force request, reaped, private payload removed |

The controller creates a normal isolated console before its async runtime initializes. Ctrl-C children inherit enabled handling without CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP; the spawned Node child has its stock process-group behavior. Ctrl-Break cases use a targeted separate parent group. The controller ignores only its own broadcast and verifies the actual Node PID after parent wait. Stdin remains open throughout. The Node fixture does not remap signals; the compiled published Moon-Lander console host owns its actual stop reason and completion. The job intentionally fails the original-Ctrl-C expectation rather than relabeling Quit as Interrupt.

## Current implementation

The Windows host binding in [3778465 process.rs](https://github.com/moonbitlang/moon/blob/3778465/crates/moonrun/src/async_sys/process.rs#L399) ignores the requested `_signal` and sends CTRL_BREAK_EVENT. The same implementation is present in [2284bd2](https://github.com/moonbitlang/moon/blob/2284bd2/crates/moonrun/src/async_sys/process.rs#L399). Its comment explains why Ctrl-Break targets a newly created process group reliably.

I understand that Windows cannot target CTRL_C_EVENT at a nonzero group and that async0.22.5 documents only SIGBREAK as supported delivery there. This is a capability request, not a suggestion to replace the constant with CTRL_C_EVENT or fake the child's reason. Is there a supported public spawn/delivery route that retains genuine Ctrl-C semantics, or can one be exposed with explicit platform capabilities?

## Small public-API reproduction

Use a private executable module importing `moonbitlang/async@0.22.5` and `moonbitlang/x@0.5.6`. Its executable package imports async, async/process, async/signal, async/stdio, core/env and x/sys.

```moonbit
async fn main {
  let args = @env.args()
  @signal.set_global_cancellation_signals([SIGINT, SIGBREAK])
  defer @async.protect_from_cancel(() => @stdio.stderr.write("PARENT_FINALIZED\n"))
  let result = @async.handle_cancellation(() => @process.run(
    args[1], args[2:].to_owned(),
    stdin=@stdio.stdin, stdout=@stdio.stdout, stderr=@stdio.stderr,
    cancel_handler=@process.graceful_cancel(timeout=2000, signal=SIGINT),
  ))
  if result is Some(code) { @sys.exit(code) }
}
```

`child.cjs`:

```js
const timer = setInterval(() => {}, 1000);
for (const [name, code] of [['SIGINT',130], ['SIGTERM',143], ['SIGBREAK',131]]) {
  process.on(name, () => {
    process.stdout.write(`CHILD_SIGNAL=${name}\n`);
    clearInterval(timer);
    process.exitCode = code;
  });
}
process.stdout.write(`CHILD_READY=${process.pid}\n`);
```

Build with `moon run --build-only --deny-warn --target wasm .`, then run the returned artifact directly with `moonrun PATH_TO_ARTIFACT -- node child.cjs` in an ordinary Windows console. After readiness, press Ctrl-C. The binding requests above select SIGINT, while the child receives SIGBREAK. Running Node directly provides the Interrupt baseline. The small public-API project compiles without warnings on both pinned stocks; the automated actual Windows records above come from the linked genuine-host fixture.

Downstream context: [Moon-Lander#140](https://github.com/moonrockz/moon-lander/issues/140). The separate [async#706](https://github.com/moonbitlang/async/issues/706) concerns observing the original signal; an observer alone cannot change this Windows delivery limitation. No hidden driver, custom toolchain, parent-death workaround or application result rewriting is requested.

Moonrun Run teardown can hang when Unix worker cancellation arrives after the cancellation check but before a blocking syscall.

`InstanceWorkers::destroy` cancels each Worker once, then calls `free_worker`/`join`. `cancel_worker` disables retry notifications. If SIGUSR2 arrives before the syscall starts, the Worker can subsequently block with no teardown-side cancellation retry to release it. This is already noted by the [FIXME in destroy](https://github.com/moonbitlang/moon/blob/9c6d5859b69dd6ad893976e951154b0fd6808183/crates/moonrun/src/async_host/workers.rs#L110-L126).

### Minimal reproducer

At `9c6d5859`, add this test inside the existing `tests` module in `crates/moonrun/src/async_sys/internal/event_loop/thread_pool/worker.rs` (it uses that module's helpers):

```rust
#[cfg(unix)]
#[test]
fn reproduce_teardown_cancel_before_read() {
    use std::io::Write;
    use std::os::fd::AsRawFd;
    use std::time::Duration;
    let (reader, mut writer) = std::io::pipe().unwrap();
    let (entered, ready) = mpsc::channel();
    let worker = spawn_worker(
        make_worker_job(7, 11),
        move |_| {
            super::super::with_cancellable_region(|| {
                entered.send(()).unwrap();
                // Widen the window after the cancellation check, before read.
                std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(200));
                let mut byte = 0u8;
                unsafe { libc::read(reader.as_raw_fd(), (&mut byte as *mut u8).cast(), 1) };
                Ok(())
            }).unwrap();
        },
        |_| {},
    );
    ready.recv_timeout(Duration::from_secs(5)).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(cancel_worker(&worker), Ok(CancellationOutcome::RetryLater));
    let (done, joined) = mpsc::channel();
    let teardown = std::thread::spawn(move || {
        free_worker(worker);
        done.send(()).unwrap();
    });
    let stalled = joined.recv_timeout(Duration::from_secs(1)).is_err();
    // Rescue the blocked read so the reproducer itself always finishes.
    writer.write_all(&[0]).unwrap();
    teardown.join().unwrap();
    assert!(stalled, "join unexpectedly finished without rescuing read");
    eprintln!("REPRODUCED: join stalled until an unrelated write released read");
}
```

Run:

```sh
cargo test -p moonrun --lib reproduce_teardown_cancel_before_read -- --nocapture
```

The sleep deliberately widens the check-to-syscall window. The test reproduces the cancel-then-join sequence used by `destroy`; the final write is only a rescue so the reproducer terminates. A passing test means the bug was observed.

**Observed:** on macOS arm64, 3/3 runs printed `REPRODUCED`: join did not complete within one second and returned after the rescue write.

**Expected:** teardown should drive cancellation retries for cancellable blocking Jobs after the guest event loop stops, so joining does not depend on unrelated I/O. This does not require forcibly terminating noncooperative computation.

