Issue found in https://github.com/moonbitlang/core/pull/2629

`moon generate-test-driver` usually generates fully-qualified names in test drivers, but the qualifiers are stripped when running within `moonbitlang/core`. This may cause issues when a `core` package shadows a builtin typename.

@bobzhang recommended that we remove the typename annotation of the static test driver maps altogether. If that's too complex, we might try decreasing the blast range of the qualifier stripping behavior to just `@moonbitlang/core/builtin` and `@moonbitlang/core/coverage` (which should theoretically be small enough for it to work).

Found in https://github.com/moonbit-community/moondiff/tree/save-problem

```moonbit
///|
test "fn insert errorpoly" {
  let edits : Array[Edit[Array[Char]]] = [
    Equal(new=[')', ' ', '-', '>', ' ', 'U'], old=[]),
    Insert(new=['?', ' ', 'r', 'a', 'i', 's', 'e']),
    Equal(new=['?', ' ', '{'], old=[]),
  ]
  @json.inspect(align_edit_boundaries(edits), content=[
    ["Equal", [], ["-", ">", " ", "U", "?"]],
    ["Insert", [" ", "r", "a", "i", "s", "e", "?"]],
    ["Equal", [], [" ", "{"]],
  ])
}
```

The JSON value `["Equal", [], ["-", ">", " ", "U", "?"]]` looks like a diff within the JSON diffing engine at https://github.com/Luni-4/json-structural-diff. This erroneously triggers a rendering when the data is *not* a diff.

cc @myfreess

